Objective:
Refactor the MW–M31 merger simulation to reduce gravitational
force-calculation complexity and improve numerical scalability, while
preserving the existing physics model and diagnostic pipeline.

Work Performed:

1. Barnes–Hut Gravity Implementation
   - Designed and implemented a Barnes–Hut octree algorithm to replace
     the direct O(N^2) pairwise Newtonian gravity solver.
   - Implemented recursive 3D spatial subdivision, node mass and
     center-of-mass calculation, and an opening-angle criterion (θ).
   - Incorporated gravitational softening consistently into the
     Barnes–Hut force evaluation.
   - Targeted effective computational scaling of approximately
     O(N log N).

2. Integration with Existing Framework
   - Encapsulated the Barnes–Hut logic in a BarnesHutSolver class that
     conforms to the existing GravitySolver interface.
   - Integrated the new solver into the leapfrog integrator without
     modifying timestep control, state-vector layout, or integration
     scheme.
   - Ensured compatibility with existing diagnostics, including
     entropy evolution, disk heating metrics, angular momentum budgets,
     and energy tracking.

3. Initial Condition Adjustments
   - Modified Hernquist halo initialization to include approximate
     isotropic velocity dispersion, reducing the severity of known
     non-equilibrium artifacts in the original zero-velocity setup.
   - Retained the existing exponential disk initialization and
     circular velocity prescription to maintain continuity with prior
     simulation configurations.

4. Code Refactoring and Readability
   - Consolidated gravity-related logic into clearly separated tree,
     node, and solver classes.
   - Preserved NumPy-only implementation constraints and avoided
     multiprocessing or external N-body libraries.
   - Maintained compatibility with the existing CenterOfMass2-based
     HST-aligned initialization workflow.

Status:
- Barnes–Hut gravity solver is fully implemented and integrated.
- No numerical validation or performance benchmarking has been
  performed yet.
- Physical fidelity, conservation properties, and runtime scaling
  remain to be tested.

Next Steps:
- Test energy and angular momentum conservation in isolated halo and
  disk systems.
- Benchmark performance against the original direct-summation solver.
- Run controlled MW–M31 merger tests to assess numerical heating and
  entropy evolution.
- Tune the Barnes–Hut opening angle θ and softening length if needed.

